Speed up HealpixSampling::selectOrientationsWithNonZeroPriorProbability

The direction loop in this function is called once per particle per class and scans the whole
sampling grid to keep about 118 directions: 12,384 candidates at healpix order 6, 49,344 at
order 7. In a RELION-5 tutorial auto-refinement it is 968.0 of the 1074.0 cumulative CPU-seconds
inside getFourierTransformsAndCtfs, 90.1% of that region and roughly 42.7 s of a 922 s
refinement. The scan grows as 4^order while the kept set stays flat, so it grows into the
largest host term on higher-resolution jobs.

Per direction the loop recomputed the prior's Euler transform, which does not depend on the
direction, rebuilt the grid direction, which does not depend on the particle, and allocated
roughly sixteen Matrix1D/Matrix2D temporaries for the symmetry search. That is ~600 ns per
direction for what is a handful of multiply-adds.

This caches the grid unit vectors and the symmetry products M_j = L_j R_j^T once per sampling
change (2.4 ms at order 7, against 22,260 particle calls per refinement) and reduces the inner
loop to nsym+1 dot products on plain doubles: 8.5-11 ns per direction, 54x faster measured
against the original function linked from the same library.

The result is bit-identical, not approximate:

  * The cache is only marked usable when every M_j is an exact signed permutation matrix. Under
    that condition L_j (d^T R_j)^T is exactly a signed component permutation of d, so the cached
    form performs the same IEEE operations rather than an algebraically equal rearrangement. Any
    other symmetry group leaves nzp_usable false and falls through to the original loop.
  * The dot products accumulate from 0 in the same index order as dotProduct().
  * Folding the operator signs into the prior is exact, since multiplying by +-1 only flips a
    sign bit.
  * ACOSD is strictly decreasing on [-1,1], so tracking the maximum dot product selects the same
    nearest direction as tracking the minimum angle, with the same first-wins tie order.
  * Membership is never decided by a cosine threshold. The cosine gate only admits candidates to
    the real ACOSD test, with a 1e-6 degree guard band, and the original `diffang < cutoff`
    comparison then decides. A boundary case cannot be dropped.

The fast path is entered only for a 3D sampling with a prior on both rot and tilt, without
symmetry relaxation, without a bimodal psi search and without the multi-body (0,90) prior.
Everything else, including the whole psi loop, is untouched.

Verified elementwise against the unpatched function over 14.8M direction evaluations at healpix
orders 6 and 7 with D2 symmetry: 0 index-set mismatches, 0 prior-value mismatches, maximum
relative difference 0.000e+00.

Applies to a clean 3dem/relion ver5.0 checkout and is independent of relion5-tt.patch. It
changes RELION's shared host code, so it speeds up every backend, CPU and GPU alike. Any
benchmark must apply it to both arms or to neither.

